n_availability_node_linked, cargo_available, not origin_availability_locked. Effect includes origin_node_occupied and origin_availability_locked. So origin_node_occupied indicates that the origin availability is locked (maybe vehicle present). Similarly load_destination_payload sets destination_node_occupied and destination_availability_locked. So origin_node_occupied is tied to availability lock, not cargo. So we can have both origin and destination availabilities locked simultaneously (each with its own lock). That does not require two cargo; locking does not consume cargo. Indeed load_origin_payload consumes cargo (makes cargo not available). load_destination_payload also consumes cargo. So we cannot lock both because cargo would be consumed twice. However maybe we can load cargo onto origin, then later transfer cargo to destination via transport leg, making cargo available again at destination? Let's see: after loading origin, cargo not available. Then we could load container onto leg etc. Eventually maybe we unload cargo at destination via unload_destination_payload? That would make cargo available again. But we need destination_node_occupied true at end, which would be set by load_destination_payload (which consumes cargo). If we unload later, destination_node_occupied would stay? Unload_destination_payload effect includes destination_node_ready, destination_availability_confirmed, cargo_available, not destination_payload_linked. It does not remove destination_node_occupied. So we could load cargo onto destination (making it occupied and cargo not available), then later unload cargo (making cargo available) but destination_node_occupied stays true. So we could have cargo initially at origin, load origin (cargo not available, origin occupied). Then we could move cargo via transport leg to destination and load destination (cargo not available, destination occupied). But we would need cargo to become available again to load destination; but after loading origin, 