n_availability_confirmed true. There is unload_destination_payload that sets destination_availability_confirmed true and cargo_available. But we want destination_node_occupied true and destination_availability_confirmed true, but cargo not available? Actually unload_destination_payload would make cargo_available, which we may not want at end (goal does not require cargo_available). But we could load destination, then not unload it; then destination_availability_confirmed would be false. However goal requires destination_availability_confirmed true. So we need to make it true. How else can we get destination_availability_confirmed true? Perhaps via some other action? Let's examine: there is no action that sets destination_availability_confirmed true except unload_destination_payload (and maybe also something else?). Check the domain: there is action load_destination_payload effect includes destination_node_occupied, destination_availability_locked, destination_payload_linked, not cargo_available. No confirmed. There is action unload_destination_payload effect includes destination_node_ready, destination_availability_confirmed, cargo_available, not destination_payload_linked. So to get destination_availability_confirmed true we need to unload destination payload (making cargo available). But goal does not require cargo_available; we could have cargo available at end? Actually goal does not list cargo_available, so it's okay if cargo_available is true at end. However goal also requires destination_node_occupied true (which remains true after unload). So we could load destination, then unload destination, making cargo available, destination confirmed, destination locked stays true, destination node occupied stays true. Then cargo becomes available at end (which is fine). However we also need origin_availability_confirmed true; we can get that via unload_origin_payload after loading origin. So overall we could: load origin, unload origin (cargo available, origin locked, 